1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,452
A language model must pick the next word. Here it has four candidates:

2
00:00:04,552 --> 00:00:05,924
cat, dog, fox, and owl.

3
00:00:06,016 --> 00:00:10,435
The model gives each candidate a score. The score is called a logit.

4
00:00:10,535 --> 00:00:12,099
Cat has the highest logit.

5
00:00:12,183 --> 00:00:15,036
Softmax turns the logits into probabilities.

6
00:00:15,136 --> 00:00:17,830
At temperature one, cat gets sixty-one percent.

7
00:00:17,916 --> 00:00:21,260
Temperature divides every logit before softmax.

8
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:24,639
At T equal to one half, the logits move apart.

9
00:00:24,739 --> 00:00:26,452
Cat now gets eighty-four percent.

10
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:28,122
Pause here and predict.

11
00:00:28,222 --> 00:00:31,706
When T rises to 2, does cat stay the most likely token?

12
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:36,522
Now raise the temperature.

13
00:00:36,622 --> 00:00:39,773
At T equal to two, the logits move together.

14
00:00:39,873 --> 00:00:41,778
The probabilities become more equal.

15
00:00:41,866 --> 00:00:43,396
Why does this happen?

16
00:00:43,496 --> 00:00:47,558
The ratio of two probabilities depends only on the distance between

17
00:00:47,658 --> 00:00:51,286
their dots. A larger distance gives a larger ratio.

18
00:00:51,386 --> 00:00:54,315
A smaller distance gives a ratio closer to one.

19
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,205
At a very low temperature, the top token takes almost all the

20
00:00:58,305 --> 00:00:59,087
probability.

21
00:00:59,187 --> 00:01:02,759
At a very high temperature, every token gets almost the same

22
00:01:02,859 --> 00:01:03,482
probability.

23
00:01:03,566 --> 00:01:06,120
In every case, the order stays the same.

24
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:09,329
Cat is always first, and owl is always last.

25
00:01:09,429 --> 00:01:11,377
Temperature changes only the spread.
